Kagami Pulse

Ingredients: J-POP + HYPNOTIC, TRANCE-LIKE & MINIMAL + Percussion  
Creative Catalyst: It was reverse-engineered from a corrupted cassette found in a time capsule.

Kagami Pulse takes the sugary, hyper-melodic hooks of late-1990s Shibuya-kei and J-pop idol music and strips them down to an obsessive, hypnotic skeleton. Bright vocal chops and pentatonic top-lines are stretched, looped, and woven into relentless, trance-inducing polyrhythmic percussion patterns. The music moves like a glass clockwork engine: hyper-precise, sparkling, yet strangely narcotic, balancing joyful pop hooks against an eerie, endless minimalist groove.

Genre DNA

- Tempo & Pulse: 122–126 BPM. The pulse relies on a rigid, motorik four-on-the-floor kick offset by interlocking interlocking woodblocks, frame drums, and hand-tuned metallic percussion that creates an illusion of accelerating time.
- Harmonic Palette: Major 7th and add9 chords derived from Japanese Shibuya-kei pop, heavily stripped of bass weight and transposed into bright, modal pentatonic loops (predominantly Lydian and Hirajoshi scales).
- Production Signatures: 
  - Tape-Warble Micro-Splicing: Vocals are chopped into micro-percussive syllables that act as secondary hi-hats, intentionally replicating wow-and-flutter dropouts.
  - Resonant Comb Filtering: Metallic percussive strikes pass through ultra-tight delay networks, turning dry hand drums into pitched, ringing bell-tones.
  - Sidechained High-Pass Gating: Bright J-pop vocal hooks breathe strictly in the spaces between complex, hypnotic percussion hits rather than driving the mix.
- Dynamics: Highly static yet emotionally intense. Tracks build through subtle additive percussion layers over eight-minute stretches, creating a hypnotic trance state that suddenly snaps into a crystal-clear, three-second J-pop chorus before dissolving back into rhythm.

Scene & Origin Lore

The genre emerged from "Roof-7," a secret late-night scene operating atop abandoned department stores in Osaka’s Minami district during the mid-2010s. Crate-digging producer Kaito "Takkyu" Mori uncovered an unmarked, sun-bleached cassette sealed inside a 1999 suburban time capsule; the magnetic tape was partially melted, crushing a pop idol single into an endless, rhythmically stuttering loop. Mori and his circle began mimicking this corrupted artifact using stripped-back hardware setups. Partygoers dressed in translucent raincoats and vintage amateur astronomy gear, gathering on damp gravel rooftops with handheld telescopes, watching the stars while swaying in place for hours to relentless, minimalist beat-craft. The ritual was equal parts star-gazing club and rhythmic trance meditation.

Instruments & Sound Engine

- Koto & Shime-daiko: Traditional Japanese percussion and plucked strings, sampled and repitched into dry, hyper-quantized polyrhythms.
- Roland CR-78 & TR-606: Vintage analog drum machines pushed through extreme delay units to provide a dry, ticking rhythmic spine.
- Yamaha DX7: Used exclusively for glassy, bell-like FM synth stabs and hyper-clean electric piano chimes.
- Micro-Chopped J-Pop Vocals: Resampled 90s vocal hooks converted into rhythmic percussion layers.

